<section class="story-section" id="problem"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p><h2>A bigger brain still<br />needs the right help.</h2><p>A delivery planner can try a hundred routes and still fail if it never receives the address. It can read yesterday’s map perfectly and still hit a gate that closed this morning.</p><p>We have met models that read more, use tools and spend longer answering. How do those pieces fit together?</p></section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea"><p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p><h2>Read. Check. Try again.</h2><p><strong>Context</strong> is the information supplied for the current answer. A <strong>tool</strong> is a program the system can call for information or an action. <strong>Inference compute</strong> is the computation spent producing an answer. These resources do different jobs.</p><p>In 2026, that combination is visible across model families. DeepSeek-V4 and V4.1-Flash support million-token contexts; a <strong>token</strong> is a piece of text. Gemini 3.1 Pro’s model card describes text, image, audio and video input. Anthropic’s Opus 5.5 announcement emphasizes tool-using work. This is a picture of converging capabilities, not a claim that the models work identically.</p><p>Keeping more context also costs memory. Our anchor paper, DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, tackles that cost by reusing and compressing stored attention information. The broader lesson: making a useful system means improving how its parts work together.</p>
<figure class="idea-diagram now-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 480 310" role="img" aria-labelledby="now-diagram-title now-diagram-desc"><title id="now-diagram-title">Three resources meet in one answer</title><desc id="now-diagram-desc">Context supplies the address, a tool supplies the gate status, and answer-time effort tries routes. A failed check can lead to another attempt. This is a conceptual illustration.</desc><g class="diagram-outline"><rect x="15" y="20" width="210" height="64" rx="8"/><rect x="255" y="20" width="210" height="64" rx="8"/><rect x="135" y="140" width="210" height="64" rx="8"/></g><g fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle"><text x="120" y="59">Context: address</text><text x="360" y="59">Tool: gate status</text><text x="240" y="179">Effort: try routes</text></g><path d="M120 90V110H360V90M240 110V132M240 210V250M232 242L240 250L248 242M350 280H440V172H353M361 164L353 172L361 180" class="diagram-line"/><rect x="135" y="255" width="210" height="48" rx="8" class="diagram-fill"/><text x="240" y="286" text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor">A checked answer</text></svg><figcaption>Information and effort are complementary. Extra attempts cannot supply a missing fact.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="story-section toy-section" id="play"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Play with it</p><h2>Get the parcel there.</h2><Discovery slug="now"/><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to assemble the planner. Send the address, fetch the closed gate’s status and try three routes to find the six-minute delivery.</p></noscript><NowToy client:load /></section>
<section class="story-section" id="mattered"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p><h2>The system is<br />part of the story.</h2><p>Look beyond the model’s name: what evidence reaches it, what can it check, and what work happens before the answer? The toy lets you separate those questions. Real systems must also learn when to use each resource, and can still make mistakes.</p><p>That brings us back to an older question: how do we make reading itself efficient? Next we rewind to the race to replace word-by-word recurrence, carrying state from one position to the next.</p><a class="next-chapter" href="/chapters/drop-recurrence/"><span>Next · 2016</span><strong>Racing to drop recurrence ↗</strong><span>Can we process a sentence without stepping through every word?</span></a></section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper"><summary>Go deeper <span>Context memory, effort and limits</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><h3>What is being compressed?</h3><p>A KV cache stores keys and values: attention representations retained for reuse while generating. It is temporary computation state, not the model’s learned weights. V4.1-Flash combines cross-layer reuse in Compressed Sparse Attention 2 with FP4 storage, a four-bit numerical format. Its causal encoder-decoder design uses different amounts of active computation when reading input and generating output.</p><h3>One result, carefully interpreted</h3><p>The report gives 890 bytes per token for its global KV cache, about one quarter of V4-Flash’s corresponding footprint. That is a specific cache comparison. It is not total device memory, an overall speedup, or proof of equal quality on every task. Persistent storage is discussed separately in the paper.</p><h3>Different resources, different costs</h3><p>For ordinary dense multi-head attention, an illustrative KV storage estimate is <code>M = 2 × L × T × Hkv × d × b / 8</code> bytes: L layers, T stored tokens, Hkv key/value heads, d values per head and b bits per value. The factor two counts keys and values. Sharing heads, compressing states or reusing them across layers changes this estimate; it does not describe V4.1-Flash’s architecture.</p><p>A longer context limit does not guarantee that a model uses every detail correctly. More answer-time computation cannot guarantee correctness either. Tool results can be stale or wrong, and an agent can choose the wrong tool. Gemini’s linked model cards note fabricated answers and practical limits; ReAct and the R1 report describe particular methods and evaluations, not universal guarantees.</p><h3>What the delivery planner does</h3><p>The planner receives the last n notes. It requires the address, then checks up to k fixed routes, stopping at the first that matches the known gate status, destination and seven-minute deadline. Without a lookup it assumes the old map’s open gate. A separate outcome check uses the actual gate state, exposing a plan that looked valid but fails. Counts show notes, local lookups and route checks separately; they are not tokens, FLOPs, latency or a cost model. No training, attention compression or language generation runs here.</p></div></details>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="sources"><summary>Sources <span>Primary papers and official model reports</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><ol class="source-list"><li><a href={paperLink('deepseek-v4-1')}>DeepSeek-AI · DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash · September 2026</a><p>Abstract and Sections 1–3: million-token context, encoder-decoder structure, cross-layer cache reuse, FP4 storage and the global-cache comparison. Section 5 discusses reasoning effort and agent evaluations. This is the chapter’s anchor paper.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('deepseek-v4')}>DeepSeek-AI · DeepSeek-V4 · 2026</a><p>Abstract and architecture sections: million-token context and compressed attention.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('gemini-3-1')}>Google DeepMind · Gemini 3.1 Pro model card · February 2026</a><p>Model Information: input modalities and context window. Its limitations section points to the <a href="https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/Model-Cards/Gemini-3-Pro-Model-Card.pdf">Gemini 3 Pro family model card</a>, whose Known Limitations section notes fabricated answers and timeouts.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('claude-opus-5-5')}>Anthropic · Introducing Claude Opus 5.5 · September 2026</a><p>Introduction and Performance: tool-using work, evaluation conditions and limits of benchmark comparisons. No cross-vendor ranking is inferred here.</p></li><li><a href={sourceLink('vaswani-2017-neurips-full-text')}>Vaswani et al. · Attention Is All You Need · 2017</a><p>Section 3: keys, values, multi-head attention and recurrence-free sequence processing. The storage expression above counts uncompressed keys and values; it is an explanatory calculation.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('yao-2022-react-synergizing-reasoning-acting-language-models')}>Yao et al. · ReAct · 2022</a><p>Method and experiments: actions return observations for subsequent decisions, with remaining failure modes.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('deepseek-ai-2025-deepseek-r1-incentivizes-reasoning-reinforcement-learning')}>DeepSeek-AI · DeepSeek-R1 · 2025</a><p>Training and evaluation sections: learning reasoning behavior and evaluating answer-time effort. This motivates the distinction between learning and trying more candidates.</p></li></ol><p>Also in this chapter: DeepSeek-V4, Gemini 3.1 Pro and Claude Opus 5.5. The synthesis, parcel, notes, map, candidate routes and diagram are teaching illustrations. They are not outputs or benchmarks from these models.</p></div></details>
